WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.234 --> 00:00:17.598
night in Dunsinane castle, a doctor and one of Lady Macbeth's serving women watched outside her bedroom.

2
00:00:18.278 --> 00:00:20.600
"How many nights has she been sleepwalking?"

3
00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:22.441
asked the doctor quietly.

4
00:00:22.461 --> 00:00:28.605
"Since the king and queen moved to the royal palace here at Dunsinane," explained the serving woman.

5
00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:36.709
"She rises from her bed, deep in sleep, and wanders the castle repeating words to herself—shhh, here she

6
00:00:36.749 --> 00:00:40.350
comes." Lady Macbeth stepped slowly out of the bedroom.

7
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:46.472
Her pale nightgown was ghostly in the moonlight, and her hair hung loose and disheveled on her shoulders.

8
00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:50.973
She kept rubbing her hands fretfully, as if she were washing them.

9
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:53.774
"Here's another spot," she mumbled.

10
00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:56.735
Then more loudly, "Out, damned spot!

11
00:00:56.915 --> 00:01:00.956
Out! Who would have thought the old king had so much blood in him?"

12
00:01:01.952 --> 00:01:02.772
"Did you hear that?"

13
00:01:03.492 --> 00:01:05.273
whispered the serving woman as they followed.

14
00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:09.554
"Banquo's buried; how can he come out of his grave?"

15
00:01:10.214 --> 00:01:11.195
continued Lady Macbeth.

16
00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:15.376
"The thane of Fife had a wife; where is she now?

17
00:01:16.036 --> 00:01:18.417
Oh, will these hands never be clean?"

18
00:01:19.357 --> 00:01:21.177
She stopped and lifted a hand to her face.

19
00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:23.838
"There's the smell of blood on them.

20
00:01:24.638 --> 00:01:28.039
All the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this hand.

21
00:01:29.059 --> 00:01:32.982
Lady Macbeth wandered back to her bedroom, still rubbing her hands in her sleep.

22
00:01:33.562 --> 00:01:34.543
The doctor shook his head.

23
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:39.828
"She needs the help of heaven more than she needs a physician," he said.

24
00:01:39.848 --> 00:01:42.771
"This disease is beyond cure."

25
00:01:43.552 --> 00:01:48.356
In the countryside in the early morning, a band of Scottish soldiers marched near Dunsinane.

26
00:01:49.157 --> 00:01:53.981
Word had spread that Malcolm and Macduff had arrived in Scotland with an army, and many who had

27
00:01:54.041 --> 00:01:56.584
suffered under Macbeth had decided to take up arms.

28
00:01:57.468 --> 00:02:01.831
A mile ahead, the soldiers could see the flags and tents of a great army camped beside a

29
00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:04.952
forest, and eagerly, they marched forward to join it.

30
00:02:06.173 --> 00:02:10.996
Inside his castle, Macbeth was pacing back and forth and raging at his servants.

31
00:02:11.016 --> 00:02:13.037
"Bring me no more reports!

32
00:02:13.577 --> 00:02:15.458
I don't care how big their army is!

33
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:20.261
Until Birnam Wood comes to Dunsinane, I'll never shake with fear.

34
00:02:21.021 --> 00:02:22.242
Who's that boy Malcolm?

35
00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:24.063
Was he not born of a woman?

36
00:02:24.423 --> 00:02:25.444
He cannot harm me!"

37
00:02:26.156 --> 00:02:28.077
The servants left him, and Macbeth sat down.

38
00:02:28.097 --> 00:02:31.440
"I am sick at heart," he mumbled.

39
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.261
"I have lived long enough.

40
00:02:33.941 --> 00:02:38.184
I can no longer hope for honor, love, or friends?only curses."

41
00:02:38.865 --> 00:02:41.867
Then, suddenly infuriated, he yelled, "Servants!

42
00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:44.189
Servants! Bring me my armor!

43
00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:47.551
I'll fight until my flesh is hacked from my bones!"

44
00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:52.734
Malcolm, Macduff, Siward, and Ross, meanwhile, were discussing the coming battle.

45
00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:57.373
They stood at the front of their camp, looking up toward the castle on Dunsinane Hill.

46
00:02:57.393 --> 00:03:02.694
"Macbeth has not deployed his army to the field," Siward was telling the others.

47
00:03:02.714 --> 00:03:07.636
"All reports say that he is staying defended inside Dunsinane Castle."

48
00:03:08.256 --> 00:03:10.897
"This gives us the advantage," said Macduff.

49
00:03:10.917 --> 00:03:13.457
"What wood is this by the camp?"

50
00:03:13.938 --> 00:03:14.458
asked Malcolm.

51
00:03:14.478 --> 00:03:17.158
"Birnam Wood," said Ross.

52
00:03:17.178 --> 00:03:22.160
"Tell every soldier to cut down a branch and carry it before him," Malcolm said.

53
00:03:22.836 --> 00:03:25.077
"This will disguise our numbers as we approach."

54
00:03:25.677 --> 00:03:28.038
The others agreed and sent word to their soldiers.

55
00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:31.880
While Macbeth was putting on his armor, a servant came rushing in.

56
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.441
"The queen, my lord, is dead!"

57
00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:35.782
cried the servant.

58
00:03:36.322 --> 00:03:37.823
"She has taken her own life!"

59
00:03:38.723 --> 00:03:40.564
Macbeth turned to him distractedly.

60
00:03:40.584 --> 00:03:43.425
"She should have died after all this was over.

61
00:03:43.825 --> 00:03:45.786
Then there would have been time for proper words."

62
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:49.768
He looked down at the pieces of his armor still waiting to be put on.

63
00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.463
Suddenly, all his ambition and wicked deeds seemed pointless and empty.

64
00:03:55.183 --> 00:03:58.645
"Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow," he sighed.

65
00:03:58.665 --> 00:04:04.989
"Life creeps in this sluggish way from day to day to the last syllable of recorded time.

66
00:04:05.609 --> 00:04:10.012
Our yesterdays just light the path to dusty death."

67
00:04:10.032 --> 00:04:13.194
He reached out to a candle and snuffed out the flame between his fingers.

68
00:04:13.214 --> 00:04:16.056
"Out, out, brief candle!

69
00:04:16.692 --> 00:04:21.274
Life is nothing but a poor actor who struts his hour upon the stage.

70
00:04:22.114 --> 00:04:25.936
It's a tale full of sound and fury, signifying nothing."

71
00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:29.137
Macbeth's thoughts were interrupted by another messenger.

72
00:04:29.157 --> 00:04:33.619
"My lord, I don't know how to report what I saw," he began.

73
00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:37.140
"Well, use your tongue," snapped Macbeth.

74
00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:43.203
"As I stood watch in the tower, I looked toward Birnam Wood, and he hesitated.

75
00:04:43.223 --> 00:04:46.284
"I thought I saw the wood begin to move."

76
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.041
"Liar!" screamed Macbeth.

77
00:04:49.061 --> 00:04:50.221
"I swear.

78
00:04:50.641 --> 00:04:55.282
Less than a mile from the castle, you can see it coming—a moving forest!"

79
00:04:56.143 --> 00:05:02.344
"If you speak falsely," said Macbeth, "I'll hang you from a tree until you starve to death!

80
00:05:02.945 --> 00:05:03.725
Sound the alarm!

81
00:05:04.065 --> 00:05:04.545
To battle!

82
00:05:04.805 --> 00:05:05.325
To battle!"

83
00:05:06.225 --> 00:05:10.527
As bells rang throughout Dunsinane Castle, trumpets rang in the field outside.

84
00:05:11.447 --> 00:05:15.048
The attacking soldiers dropped their camouflage of tree branches and charged.

85
00:05:16.136 --> 00:05:19.477
The battle was grisly, but Macbeth's soldiers fought without heart.

86
00:05:20.457 --> 00:05:25.159
Malcolm's soldiers, on the other hand, had the courage of hope and battled their way inside the castle.

87
00:05:26.219 --> 00:05:29.720
In the main courtyard, ferocious Macbeth fought on, undefeated.

88
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.501
Fallen soldiers lay on the ground all around him.

89
00:05:33.481 --> 00:05:36.582
Siward, seeing him, called out, "There's the tyrant!"

90
00:05:37.082 --> 00:05:37.862
and led a charge.

91
00:05:38.462 --> 00:05:41.363
Their swords clashed briefly before Macbeth struck him down.

92
00:05:41.383 --> 00:05:44.304
"Here lies a man born of a woman!"

93
00:05:46.356 --> 00:05:49.098
laughed Macbeth, pulling his sword from Siward's side.

94
00:05:49.818 --> 00:05:53.321
From behind him, a voice yelled, "Turn, you hellhound, turn!"

95
00:05:54.201 --> 00:05:55.942
Macbeth spun around to face Macduff.

96
00:05:56.883 --> 00:05:58.704
"You should avoid me, Macduff.

97
00:05:59.465 --> 00:06:01.966
I have enough of your family's blood on my hands."

98
00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:07.650
"I have no words for you," answered Macduff, swinging at his adversary.

99
00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.152
"My voice is in my sword!"

100
00:06:11.072 --> 00:06:16.156
The clang of their weapons rang loudly above the battle as Macbeth struck back, blow for blow.

101
00:06:17.184 --> 00:06:21.127
"You can make me bleed as easily as you can hurt the air," he said.

102
00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:26.210
"I lead a charmed life; I cannot fall to any man of woman born!"

103
00:06:27.431 --> 00:06:29.432
Macduff forced Macbeth's sword aside.

104
00:06:29.452 --> 00:06:31.614
"Then your charm has abandoned you!"

105
00:06:32.154 --> 00:06:32.535
he said.

106
00:06:32.555 --> 00:06:36.877
"My mother died as I was born, and a surgeon cut me from her womb!"

107
00:06:37.838 --> 00:06:39.119
Macbeth's face went white.

108
00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:42.661
"The witches have tricked me by juggling words!"

109
00:06:42.681 --> 00:06:46.624
"All hope for you is gone," said Macduff.

110
00:06:46.684 --> 00:06:47.645
"Surrender now."

111
00:06:47.665 --> 00:06:50.007
"I will not yield!

112
00:06:50.548 --> 00:06:53.050
Nor kiss the ground before Malcolm's feet!

113
00:06:53.731 --> 00:06:58.915
Though Birnam Wood has come to Dunsinane, though a man not born of a woman opposes me, I

114
00:06:58.955 --> 00:07:00.917
will fight to my last breath."

115
00:07:01.858 --> 00:07:03.900
Macbeth gripped his sword with both hands.

116
00:07:04.901 --> 00:07:09.204
"Lay on, Macduff, and damned be the first of us to cry for mercy!"

117
00:07:10.786 --> 00:07:16.190
Macduff lunged at Macbeth with a cry, and the two fought on among the struggling men and flashing

118
00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.651
weapons in combat around them.

119
00:07:18.452 --> 00:07:21.394
Malcolm entered the castle with Ross, accompanied by guards.

120
00:07:21.914 --> 00:07:23.255
The battle was going in their favor.

121
00:07:24.436 --> 00:07:28.959
In fact, many of Macbeth's soldiers had abandoned him and turned to fight with Malcolm's men.

122
00:07:28.979 --> 00:07:33.242
"It looks as though our victory is cheaply bought," said Malcolm.

123
00:07:33.262 --> 00:07:35.483
"Where are Siward and Macduff?"

124
00:07:36.344 --> 00:07:39.566
"Macduff is missing, and Siward is slain," said Ross.

125
00:07:39.586 --> 00:07:42.628
"God be with him," Malcolm said.

126
00:07:43.092 --> 00:07:44.793
Just then, Macduff approached.

127
00:07:45.613 --> 00:07:48.294
Held high on the point of his sword was Macbeth's head.

128
00:07:48.314 --> 00:07:51.135
"Behold the tyrant's cursed head!"

129
00:07:51.736 --> 00:07:52.416
cried Macduff.

130
00:07:53.016 --> 00:07:54.817
"Scotland is free!"

131
00:07:55.597 --> 00:08:00.179
And all around, the injured and weary soldiers put down their weapons and cheered.

132
00:08:00.959 --> 00:08:02.400
Malcolm quietly bowed his head.

133
00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.282
It would take much work to undo the tragedy Macbeth had brought to his land.
